      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel A — Reed-Sternberg Cell",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>The diagnostic cell of classical Hodgkin lymphoma: a large <strong>binucleate</strong> cell with mirror-image nuclei, each containing a single huge <strong>eosinophilic 'owl-eye' nucleolus</strong>. Immunophenotype <strong>CD15+ / CD30+ / CD45−</strong>. RS cells are sparse within a reactive background.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "79f7405c-4db2-4696-a8b9-b6c2b43180fe",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel A — Reed-Sternberg Cell"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 32,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel B — Lacunar Cell",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>A Hodgkin-cell variant sitting in an empty <strong>lacuna</strong> (clear space from formalin retraction artefact). Characteristic of the <strong>nodular sclerosis</strong> subtype — the commonest classical HL, typically young women with mediastinal disease.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "0410fb81-58d6-4e19-8714-1f6aa485a865",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel B — Lacunar Cell"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel C — Nodular Sclerosis",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>Low power: broad <strong>collagen bands</strong> divide the lymph node into cellular <strong>nodules</strong>. Defines the <strong>nodular sclerosis</strong> subtype. The fibrous bands + lacunar cells are the diagnostic pairing.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "ac7d5cf5-148c-4036-9e6e-b01e2c72c7bb",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel C — Nodular Sclerosis"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel D — Mixed Inflammatory Background",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>The polymorphous reactive milieu around RS cells: small <strong>lymphocytes</strong>, <strong>eosinophils</strong>, plasma cells and histiocytes. Prominent in the <strong>mixed cellularity</strong> subtype. This reactive background (not the sparse RS cells) makes up most of the tumour bulk.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "0be8e4fa-86ca-420a-9387-eb9a35eef2d2",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel D — Mixed Inflammatory Background"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
